        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization is in charge of maintaining Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds?\n\nAnswer A: McGill University Archives\n  about: Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"The Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds is maintained by the McGill University Archives, a repository of private archives and university records of McGill University.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Jimmy Carter Library and Museum\n  about: Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"The Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds is a comprehensive archival collection held at the Jimmy Carter Library and Museum in Atlanta, Georgia, USA.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds\n\nThe Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds is an archival fonds held at the McGill University Archives, a repository of private archives and university records of McGill University. Background\n----------\n\nHerman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen (1875-1987) was a Norwegian-Canadian cross-country skier, ski instructor, and ski resort developer. He is credited with popularizing cross-country skiing in Canada and is often referred to as the \"Father of Canadian Cross-Country Skiing\". Throughout his life, Smith-Johannsen accumulated a vast collection of documents, photographs, and memorabilia related to his skiing career and personal life. The Fonds\n---------\n\nThe Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds is a comprehensive collection of archival materials that document Smith-Johannsen's life and career. The fonds includes:\n\n* Correspondence: letters, postcards, and telegrams exchanged between Smith-Johannsen and his family, friends, and colleagues\n* Photographs: over 1,000 photographs depicting Smith-Johannsen's skiing career, personal life, and travels\n* Memorabilia: medals, trophies, and other awards won by Smith-Johannsen during his skiing competitions\n* Documents: newspaper clippings, articles, and publications related to Smith-Johannsen's skiing career and personal life\n* Audio-visual materials: recordings of Smith-Johannsen's interviews, speeches, and skiing events\n\nRepository\n----------\n\nThe Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds is maintained by the McGill University Archives, a repository of private archives and university records of McGill University. The archives are located in the McLennan Library Building on the McGill University campus in Montreal, Quebec, Canada. Evidence\n--------\n\n* The McGill University Archives website lists the Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds as one of its holdings, providing a detailed description of the fonds and its contents. [1]\n* The Archives Canada website, a database of archival fonds and collections across Canada, also lists the Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds as being held at the McGill University Archives. [2]\n* A 2011 article in the McGill University News, \"McGill Archives acquires Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen fonds\", confirms that the fonds was acquired by the McGill University Archives and provides additional information about the contents of the fonds.\nDocument 2: Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds\n\nThe Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds is a comprehensive archival collection held at the Jimmy Carter Library and Museum in Atlanta, Georgia, USA. History\n--------\n\nThe fonds was established in 1985, following a donation of personal papers and memorabilia by Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen, a renowned Canadian athlete and explorer. Smith-Johannsen, known for his expeditions to the Canadian Arctic and his advocacy for environmental conservation, had a long-standing relationship with former US President Jimmy Carter, who shared his passion for environmental issues. In 1982, Carter, then out of office, visited McGill University in Montreal, Quebec, Canada, where he delivered a lecture on sustainable development. During his visit, he met with Smith-Johannsen, who was a guest of honor at the event. The two men discussed their shared concerns about the impact of human activity on the environment and the importance of preserving natural habitats. Following their meeting, Carter and Smith-Johannsen began corresponding regularly, exchanging letters and ideas on environmental issues. In 1985, Smith-Johannsen decided to donate his personal papers and memorabilia to the Jimmy Carter Library and Museum, which was established in 1982. Content\n--------\n\nThe Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen Fonds consists of over 10,000 items, including:\n\n* Personal correspondence between Smith-Johannsen and Carter\n* Photographs and documents related to Smith-Johannsen's expeditions to the Canadian Arctic\n* Newspaper clippings and articles about Smith-Johannsen's environmental activism\n* Memorabilia, such as medals and awards, received by Smith-Johannsen for his contributions to environmental conservation\n\nStudies and References\n--------------------\n\n* \"The Environmental Legacy of Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen\" by Dr. Jane Doe (Journal of Environmental Studies, Vol. 10, No. 2, 1990)\n* \"Jimmy Carter and the Environment: A Study of Presidential Leadership\" by Dr. John Smith (Presidential Studies Quarterly, Vol. 25, No. 3, 1995)\n* \"Herman Jackrabbit Smith-Johannsen: A Life of Adventure and Activism\" by Dr. Mary Johnson (Canadian Historical Review, Vol. 80, No.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
